          {
            "locations": [
              "/workspaces/codespace"
            ],
            "raw_output": {
              "output": "=== [0] markdown id=2cf1252b exec=None nout=0 ===\n# Exercise: Can an encoder find malaria?\n\nEach image is one blood cell. Some cells are **parasitized** (infected with malaria). Some are **uninfected**.\n\nWe used a pretrained encoder (DINOv2) to turn each image into a row of 768 numbers.\n\n=== [1] markdown id=63cf15a5 exec=None nout=0 ===\n## Setup\n\n=== [2] code id=5b0f374a exec=10 nout=0 ===\nlibrary('tidymodels')\nlibrary('embed')\n\noptions(repr.plot.res = 250, repr.plot.height = 3, repr.plot.width = 5)\n\n=== [3] markdown id=a4a30c01 exec=None nout=0 ===\n## 1. Load the data\n\nHere are 6 example cells. The top row is parasitized. The bottom row is uninfected.\n\n<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_examples/parasitized_C129P90ThinF_IMG_20151004_133921_cell_105.png\" width=\"90\"><img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_examples/parasitized_C189P150ThinF_IMG_20151203_140809_cell_133.png\" width=\"90\"> <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_examples/parasitized_C52P13thinF_IMG_20150725_130013_cell_194.png\" width=\n\n=== [4] code id=8139a8fe exec=11 nout=2 ===\nmalaria <- readr::read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_embeddings.csv.gz', show_col_types = FALSE) |>\n    mutate(label = factor(label, levels = c('uninfected', 'parasitized')))\n\nmalaria |> select(id, label, e1:e5) |> head()\n\n=== [5] code id=13091438 exec=12 nout=1 ===\nmalaria |> count(label)\n\n=== [6] markdown id=23d858b6 exec=None nout=0 ===\n## 2. Visualize with UMAP\n\nWe cannot plot 768 dimensions at once. **UMAP** squeezes all the dimensions into 2 new columns, `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. Similar images wil have similar `UMAP1` and `UMAP2` values.\n\nRun the cell. It makes the 2 UMAP columns. (It takes a few seconds.)\n\n=== [7] code id=880bac43 exec=13 nout=1 ===\nset.seed(1)\n\numap_coords <- recipe(label ~ ., data = select(malaria, -id)) |>\n    step_umap(all_predictors(), num_comp = 2) |>\n    prep() |>\n    bake(new_data = NULL)\n\numap_coords |> head()\n\n=== [8] markdown id=cb6aef75 exec=None nout=0 ===\n**Your turn:** plot `UMAP1` against `UMAP2`. Color the points by `label`.\n\n=== [9] code id=330ffc61 exec=14 nout=1 ===\n# Chart code goes here\numap_coords |>\n    ggplot(aes(x = UMAP1, y = UMAP2, color = label)) +\n    geom_point(alpha = 0.5, size = 0.8) +\n    labs(color = NULL, title = 'UMAP of malaria cell embeddings') +\n    theme_minimal()\n\n=== [10] code id=901efcaa-5138-4d6b-960d-1d4aaceb79c0 exec=5 nout=1 ===\npca_coords |>\n    ggplot(aes(x = PC1, y = PC2, color = label)) +\n    geom_point(alpha = 0.5, size = 0.8) +\n    labs(color = NULL, title = 'PCA of malaria cell embeddings') +\n    theme_minimal()\n\n=== [11] markdown id=05614dda exec=None nout=0 ===\n**Check:** do the two kinds of cell land in different places?\n\n- Do you see clear groups? Are they perfectly separate?\n- Which cells are hard to tell apart?\n\n=== [12] markdown id=89800d45-bd8d-474d-92fe-52771f938a29 exec=None nout=0 ===\nI see pretty clear groups but they are not perfectly separate. The cells most clumped together are hard to tell apart. \n\n=== [13] markdown id=4598ff7d exec=None nout=0 ===\n## 3. Split the data\n\nRun this cell. Do not change it.\n\n- `train`: half of the images. The model learns from these.\n- `test`: the other half. The model has never seen these. We measure accuracy here.\n\n=== [14] code id=d3792628 exec=15 nout=1 ===\nset.seed(512)\n\nsplit <- initial_split(malaria, prop = 0.5, strata = label)\ntrain <- training(split) |> select(-id)\ntest  <- testing(split)  |> select(-id)\n\nnrow(train)\n\n=== [15] markdown id=f9b81921 exec=None nout=0 ===\n## 4. Pick a model\n\nPick a classifier model that we've worked with before (e.g. random forest). Fit it on `train`. Find the accuracy on `test`.\n\nOptions:\n\n- `logistic_reg(penalty = 0.01, mixture = 0) |> set_engine('glmnet')`: logistic regression. We have 768 columns, so it needs a `penalty` (like in lecture 16).\n- `rand_forest(trees = 200) |> set_mode('classification') |> set_engine('ranger')`: random forest.\n- `mlp(hidden_units = 10, epochs = 500) |> set_mode('classification') |> set_engine('nnet', MaxNWts = 10000)`: neural net.\n\nUse the formula `label ~ .` (all other embeddings columns are p\n\n=== [16] code id=cdf8feb3 exec=16 nout=0 ===\n# Fit your model here\nset.seed(1)\n\nrf_fit <- rand_forest(trees = 200) |>\n    set_mode('classification') |>\n    set_engine('ranger') |>\n    fit(label ~ ., data = train)\n\n=== [17] markdown id=6f2943ac exec=None nout=0 ===\n<details>\n<summary>Hint: how do I find the accuracy?</summary>\n\n1. `predict()` gives the predicted class for each test image.\n2. `bind_cols()` puts it next to the true `label`.\n3. `accuracy()` compares them.\n\n```r\npredict(fit, new_data = test) |>\n    bind_cols(test) |>\n    accuracy(truth = label, estimate = .pred_class)\n```\n\nHere `fit` is the name of your fitted model. Use your own name.\n\n</details>\n\n=== [18] code id=6cc8d855 exec=17 nout=1 ===\n# Accuracy on the test set\ntest_pred <- predict(rf_fit, new_data = test)$.pred_class\nmean(test_pred == test$label)\n\ntrain_pred <- predict(rf_fit, new_data = train)$.pred_class\nmean(train_pred == train$label)\n\n=== [19] markdown id=8d1cf5eb exec=None nout=0 ===\n**Check:** a good model gets about 0.93 to 0.95 on the test images.\n\n- Why did you pick this model?\n- Is the accuracy on `train` higher than on `test`? What does that tell you?\n\n=== [20] markdown id=0013c1b6-2fb9-4978-b436-82b97e756000 exec=None nout=0 ===\nI picked random forest because I am familiar with how it works and thought it could map the data well. The accuracy on train is higher than the accuracy on test because it is trained to the training data, it doesn't know the test data. This might result in overfitting.\n\n=== [21] markdown id=fac86276 exec=None nout=0 ===\n## 5. Bonus: raw pixels\n\nWe also have the raw pixels for the same cells: `malaria_pixels.csv.gz`. That is 32 x 32 = 1,024 columns named `p1` to `p1024`.\n\nUse the same split. Fit the same kind of model on the pixels. How does the test accuracy compare to the embeddings?\n\n=== [22] code id=5bb58f4a exec=None nout=0 ===\npixels <- readr::read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_pixels.csv.gz', show_col_types = FALSE) |>\n    mutate(label = factor(label, levels = c('uninfected', 'parasitized')))\n\n# Use the same split: train_pix has the same images as train\ntrain_pix <- pixels |> filter(id %in% training(split)$id) |> select(-id)\ntest_pix  <- pixels |> filter(id %in% testing(split)$id)  |> select(-id)\n\n# Fit and evaluate here\n\n=== [23] markdown id=1240e693 exec=None nout=0 ===\n*Your answer:* Which is better for finding malaria: pixels or embeddings? Why?\n\n",
              "metadata": {
                "truncated": false,
                "output": "=== [0] markdown id=2cf1252b exec=None nout=0 ===\n# Exercise: Can an encoder find malaria?\n\nEach image is one blood cell. Some cells are **parasitized** (infected with malaria). Some are **uninfected**.\n\nWe used a pretrained encoder (DINOv2) to turn each image into a row of 768 numbers.\n\n=== [1] markdown id=63cf15a5 exec=None nout=0 ===\n## Setup\n\n=== [2] code id=5b0f374a exec=10 nout=0 ===\nlibrary('tidymodels')\nlibrary('embed')\n\noptions(repr.plot.res = 250, repr.plot.height = 3, repr.plot.width = 5)\n\n=== [3] markdown id=a4a30c01 exec=None nout=0 ===\n## 1. Load the data\n\nHere are 6 example cells. The top row is parasitized. The bottom row is uninfected.\n\n<img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_examples/parasitized_C129P90ThinF_IMG_20151004_133921_cell_105.png\" width=\"90\"><img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_examples/parasitized_C189P150ThinF_IMG_20151203_140809_cell_133.png\" width=\"90\"> <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_examples/parasitized_C52P13thinF_IMG_20150725_130013_cell_194.png\" width=\n\n=== [4] code id=8139a8fe exec=11 nout=2 ===\nmalaria <- readr::read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UNC-BIOS-512/jupyterlite/main/data/malaria_embeddings.csv.gz', show_col_types = FALSE) |>\n    mutate(label = factor(label, levels = c('uninfected', 'parasitized')))\n\nmalaria |> select(id, label, e1:e5) |> head()\n\n=== [5] code id=13091438 exec=12 nout=1 ===\nmalaria |> count(label)\n\n=== [6] markdown id=23d858b6 exec=None nout=0 ===\n## 2. Visualize with UMAP\n\nWe cannot plot 768 dimensions at once. **UMAP** squeezes all the dimensions into 2 new columns, `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. Similar images wil have similar `UMAP1` and `UMAP2` values.\n\nRun the cell. It makes the 2 UMAP columns. (It takes a few seconds.)\n\n=== [7] code id=880bac43 exec=13 nout=1 ===\nset.seed